 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Instead of steps 1–5: simmer <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"560\" data-s=\"0\">560 ml</span> water, <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"80\" data-s=\"0\">80 ml</span> mentsuyu and <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tbsp\" data-a=\"1\" data-s=\"0\">1 tbsp</span> mirin, then cover and turn off the heat.</li><li>That’s JOC’s 1 : 7 dilution — check your bottle’s ratio for hot soba (kaketsuyu).</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Quick broth with mentsuyu"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Any good dashi works as the base: <a href=\"shiitake-kombu-dashi.html\">shiitake–kombu</a> (vegetarian) or <a href=\"niboshi-bonito-dashi.html\">niboshi–bonito</a>.</li><li>Use <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"720\" data-s=\"0\">720 ml</span> and season as in step 5.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Other dashi"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Diamond Crystal kosher salt is light and flaky; <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tsp\" data-a=\"0.5\" data-s=\"0\">½ tsp</span> fine salt is roughly the same saltiness (a conversion, not JOC’s).</p>",
   "title_html": "Salt"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Don’t throw them out — they make <a href=\"furikake.html\">furikake</a>.</p>",
   "title_html": "Spent kombu and bonito"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Stop at the kombu dashi (skip the bonito), and skip the tempura and kamaboko.</p>",
   "title_html": "Vegetarian"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Keep broth, noodles and toppings separately, up to 2 days in the fridge.</p>",
   "title_html": "Leftovers"
  }
 ],
